<!--
  A Gmail filter that labels shipping mail `parcels`, in Gmail's own export format.

  Import it at Settings, Filters and Blocked Addresses, Import filters, and TICK
  "Also apply filter to matching conversations" - import by itself labels nothing that has already
  arrived, so without that tick the app sees an empty label and cannot tell that from a broken one.
  Then Settings, Labels, tick "Show in IMAP" for `parcels`, or IMAP cannot see it at all.

  The whole query is ONE property, `hasTheWord`, and that is the entire point of this file.
  Gmail's filter FORM has separate From / Subject / Has-the-words boxes and it ANDs them together,
  so a filter built as From: the carriers AND Subject: a dispatch word matches nothing: no single
  message satisfies both. The query below is one OR-expression in one field, and uses no `from:` or
  `subject:` operator at all. Bare terms match anywhere in the message, headers included, so a
  carrier domain still matches mail from that carrier - and also matches an e-shop that merely names
  the carrier, which is wanted here.

  It covers the five carriers and e-shop dispatch wording, because the shop's own "your order has
  been dispatched" mail is often the FIRST notice of a parcel, days before the carrier writes, and
  sometimes the only one. Gmail ignores diacritics (odoslana matches odoslaná) but does not stem, so
  the word forms are listed one by one.

  Edit it before or after importing: add the domains of the shops you actually order from. There is
  deliberately no "never send to spam" action - mail claiming a parcel is waiting is one of the most
  common phishing templates in this region, and exempting a broad query like this from the spam
  filter would be a poor trade. Mail this labels that is not a parcel costs one click on /attention.
-->
